WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
A drug that blocks anchor installation leaves newly made proteins without grip.

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.720
But a cell can also move a protein without changing its anchor.

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.320
How does a protein choose one membrane region over another?

4
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.240
The membrane itself can carry a changing address.

5
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.660
Phosphoinositides are membrane lipids with head groups facing the cytosol.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.800
Enzymes can add or remove phosphates from those heads.

7
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:37.000
The tails stay buried, while the exposed head-group pattern changes what cytosolic

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.400
proteins can recognize.

9
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:42.563
In this pathway, PI3-kinase is the writer.

10
00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:49.383
When recruited and activated, it converts membrane PIP2 into PIP3.

11
00:00:50.863 --> 00:00:55.383
Production can be local, so one membrane region gains the address before another.

12
00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:59.677
PIP3 does not stay under one enzyme.

13
00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:04.817
Lipids diffuse sideways, while phosphatases remove the mark.

14
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:10.077
The visible pattern reflects production, spread, and removal together.

15
00:01:11.357 --> 00:01:12.857
The address can therefore move.

16
00:01:14.077 --> 00:01:17.157
It is a dynamic address, not a fixed postal code.

17
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:21.547
A blue reporter now floats beneath two membrane regions.

18
00:01:22.867 --> 00:01:25.147
Only one region contains PIP3.

19
00:01:26.487 --> 00:01:27.987
Where should a matching reader accumulate?

20
00:01:29.447 --> 00:01:31.687
Keep watching the reporter, not just the lipid.

21
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.890
The reporter accumulates at the PIP3 region.

22
00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:46.330
Its reader is a pleckstrin homology domain, shortened to PH domain.

23
00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:52.370
Drawn as a cup, it grips a compatible lipid head group and recruits the attached

24
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:53.370
protein.

25
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.663
Akt uses such a domain.

26
00:01:57.023 --> 00:02:00.863
Before its matching lipid appears, Akt can remain in the cytosol.

27
00:02:02.143 --> 00:02:05.903
When the address rises, Akt is recruited to that membrane region.

28
00:02:07.163 --> 00:02:11.123
A green fluorescent PH-domain fusion makes this movement visible.

29
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:14.177
PTEN is an eraser.

30
00:02:15.517 --> 00:02:20.177
It removes the phosphate that distinguishes PIP3 from PIP2.

31
00:02:21.357 --> 00:02:26.217
As the address fades, the matching readers lose that contact and return toward the

32
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:27.217
cytosol.

33
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.740
The reporter is evidence, not a direct photograph of one lipid.

34
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.460
Its binding preference, competing contacts, and cell shape can affect the glow.

35
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.920
Controls must test the binding pocket, the writer, and imaging geometry.

36
00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:46.863
This is the address half of the lesson's rule.

37
00:02:48.363 --> 00:02:53.443
Lipids control signaling protein location by supplying grip, address, or both.

38
00:02:55.103 --> 00:02:59.423
Writing and erasing the lipid pattern changes which readers gather at a membrane.

39
00:03:00.567 --> 00:03:02.847
So read a lipid signal as a process.

40
00:03:04.287 --> 00:03:08.947
Ask where the writer acts, which domain recognizes the product, and which eraser

41
00:03:08.947 --> 00:03:14.167
removes it. Those three answers predict the changing recruitment pattern.
